{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 argues that 'The complete evidence requirement for distinguishing truly independent failures from cryptic common-cause failures is the monitoring of the independent-causal-pathway supply chains.' But statistical independence tests can also provide distinguishing evidence: the problem asks to 'Identify what evidence would distinguish failure modes that are truly independent from failures that merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found.' A shared common-cause failure typically produces a statistically detectable spike in correlated failures during perturbation events. SRC_001's claim of completeness incorrectly excludes statistical/observational evidence as a partial distinguishing mechanism, demanding absolute supply-chain attestation instead.","counterexample":["A network of decentralized nodes shares a common cloud provider. Statistical monitoring of failure times reveals failures cluster tightly during cloud-provider outages and are otherwise independent. This statistical evidence distinguishes the cryptic common-cause mode from true independence without complete supply-chain auditing."],"target":"52e5200b094c58c5155820818c4f221ebc20a7c9960bf979711ffacb8981ce4f"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's mechanism claims 'neither architecture can withstand the loss of a shared external dependency,' but its second countercondition posits that 'The large service's internal redundancy includes independent replicas of all external dependencies, achieving strict isolation.' If the large service replicates all external dependencies internally (e.g., running its own DNS, certificate authority, etc.), it CAN withstand the loss of external shared dependencies that would cascade through the decentralized network. By flatly asserting 'neither architecture can withstand the loss of a shared external dependency,' SRC_002 contradicts its own countercondition and fails to address how the two architectures compare when the large service strictly isolates itself from external dependencies.","counterexample":["A large service runs its own internal DNS, certificate authority, and redundant power supplies, while a decentralized network of small services relies on a shared external cloud provider. The external provider fails. The large service withstands the failure; the decentralized network does not. This directly contradicts SRC_002's claim that 'neither architecture can withstand the loss of a shared external dependency.'"],"target":"f163136e707aafe104474fdea1043aabb7d39d31834242ca15642aab5948b572"}]}